    "rationale": "Evidence from iteration 1 (entityR2A): oval finishes 20/20 (mean progress 0.997, max_error 0.025). s-bend finishes 8/20 and fails off track at mean progress 0.83 (max_error 0.051). Slalom fails 20/20 at mean progress 0.18 (max_error 0.064, 2 line losses per run). Iteration 0 failed everywhere at progress 0.10 to 0.16, so the 16 mm footprint and 12 mm spacing helped. The failure mode is still drift past the 0.06 m off-track limit on sharper bends, not oscillation (steering_oscillation is 0.23 to 0.31). Body width cannot help: the limit is max(0.06, 0.0125 + body_width/4), and that stays at the 0.06 m floor for any body_width up to 0.18 m. Body length has no dynamic effect. So the only geometry levers are the sensor array and the drivetrain. Changes: (1) Reduce wheel_track from 0.12 m to 0.10 m. Turn rate is wheel speed difference divided by track, so a narrower track gives more yaw per unit of PWM difference, which should help the tight slalom bends. (2) Widen the sensor array from +/-0.024 m to +/-0.03 m, with 15 mm spacing, so the outer sensors still see the tape when the robot has drifted about 0.04 m. The 16 mm footprint is kept, so adjacent sensors still overlap the 25 mm tape. Sensor weights stay at [2, 1, 0, -1, -2], scaled to the new spacing. (3) Keep mass 0.5 kg, wheel_radius 0.03 m, sensors at y = 0.05 m, and body dimensions unchanged, so the only changes are the array width and the track width. This isolates the effect of turn authority and capture range. Expected result: slalom and s-bend completion rise, with max_error falling below 0.06 m on at least some slalom seeds.",
